.TH PROTO-SYSEXT 8 "2026-10-26" "Proto" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
proto-sysext \- signed extension repositories and lockstep OS features
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B proto-sysext
.RB [ --repo " ID"]
.RB [ --json ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ NAME ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Discovers extension images for the running desktop, architecture and exact OS
image version using authenticated SHA256SUMS listings. Lower numeric repository
priorities take precedence. Equal-priority providers for the same extension are
an error. Authentication or network failures are not silently bypassed by
falling back to a lower-priority repository.
.PP
Native transfer definitions are the source of truth for the configured update
source. Feature definitions and their drop-ins determine enablement; downloaded
versions are read from the image directory. There is no persistent installation
database. These describe current configuration, not proof of which server
historically supplied existing image bytes.
.PP
HTTPS source URLs are normalized for hostname case, default port 443 and trailing
slashes, then matched against repository configuration. All aliases for an equal
URL are reported. A source without a matching repository is reported as
unconfigured. Redirects and shared signing keys do not imply equal repository
identity. The same URL under a different alias is not a vendor change.
.PP
An enabled or downloaded extension stays with its configured URL during an
ordinary install, even when a higher-priority provider becomes available.
Repository disablement excludes discovery; changing or removing native transfer
configuration is a separate operation.
.SH COMMANDS
.PP
Commands that change persistent configuration, images, activation or catalog
caches first display a categorized change summary and require confirmation.
Enter y to proceed, n (or Enter) to cancel, or d to display detailed changes
and return to the prompt. Non-interactive mutations require explicit approval
options. Dry runs and
unchanged installations do not prompt. Signing-key imports are separate shared
trust changes with their own summary, details and confirmation; an approved key
import is retained even if a later installation is cancelled. Installation
discovery does not update the persistent catalog cache; explicit refresh does.
.PP
-y or --assume-yes accepts transaction confirmations automatically, while still
printing the summary. It does not accept unknown signing keys. --accept-keys
separately accepts new repository signing keys into the shared systemd trust
store after verifying the fetched key against the catalog signature. This grants
system-wide trust and does not independently authenticate the key's owner.
Use both options for unattended changes that may introduce new trusted keys.
Either option may appear before or after the command. --accept-keys does not
imply --assume-yes. Dry runs never import keys, even with both options.
.TP
.B repos
Lists configured repositories, priorities, enablement and public-key URLs without downloading.
.TP
.B refresh
Requires root. Refreshes and caches authenticated repository listings. Unknown
signers trigger interactive key review; approval imports the key into systemd's
shared trust store. Already trusted signers do not prompt again. Non-interactive
refresh only accepts an unknown key when --accept-keys was explicitly supplied.
.TP
.BI "repo add " "ID URL"
Requires root. Adds local repository configuration. --priority selects the
numeric priority; --key-url specifies a public-key location. --replace explicitly
overrides an existing definition. Adding a repository does not trust its key.
.TP
.BI "repo remove " ID
Requires root. Masks the repository with a local /dev/null symlink, including
vendor definitions. Refuses removal while the current deployment has registered
extensions using that URL and no remaining alias for the URL exists. Shared
trusted keys are not revoked.
.TP
.B list
Lists compatible extensions from verified repository listings together with
their configured source and preferred available provider. --installed-only
restricts the result to enabled or downloaded extensions.
.TP
.BI search " TEXT"
Filters list output by an extension-name substring. Supports --installed-only.
.TP
.BI info " NAME"
Downloads the optional per-image JSON, verifies its checksum against the signed
listing, and displays compatibility and package information.
.TP
.B installed
Reads owned effective feature/transfer definitions and downloaded image names.
--all also includes disabled definitions without images. Drop-ins are read in
native directory/filename precedence; manually edited source URLs and feature
enablement are reflected without another registration record.
.TP
.BI install " NAME"
Requires root. Downloads using systemd-pull with the authenticated file digest,
checks embedded release metadata and Verity through systemd-dissect, installs
the read-only image, creates native feature/transfer definitions, and refreshes
systemd-sysext. Same-source images already present are reused without overwriting.
--install-from REPO (alias --from) selects a source explicitly, regardless of
priority and forces a fresh verified installation even when the URL is unchanged.
Replacing an enabled/downloaded extension's source additionally
requires --allow-vendor-change. That option without --install-from permits
selection of the best-priority compatible provider. Vendor means the normalized
source URL here, not the RPM Vendor field.
.TP
.BI switch-repo " REPO"
Explicitly switches all currently enabled owned extensions also published for
the running image in REPO. Others are reported as skipped and not removed.
All replacements are downloaded and verified before any image/configuration
changes. The operation itself grants vendor-change permission.
.PP
install, remove and switch-repo accept -D or --dry-run. A dry run reports the
plan without changing files, importing keys or downloading image payloads.
Uncached catalog retrieval may still require the network; unknown keys require
a separate interactive refresh. Read-only dry runs do not require root.
.PP
Vendor switches replace directory entries atomically rather than overwriting
mounted image inodes. The previous bytes/configuration are held temporarily
until a forced systemd-sysext refresh succeeds; ordinary command failures restore
them and retry the old merge. This is not a durable power-loss transaction journal.
Administrator drop-ins that override the requested source/enablement cause a
failure and rollback rather than a silent partial switch. Unmanaged files are
not adopted. Modifications to transfer ownership patterns/target paths are
rejected instead of guessing which files can be replaced or deleted.
.TP
.BI remove " NAME"
Requires root. Disables the owned feature, invokes sysupdate vacuum with only
that extension's definitions, and refreshes systemd-sysext. Disabled definitions
remain available for ownership tracking. All versions of that feature may be
removed, including rollback versions. Unmanaged images/configuration are not
adopted or overwritten.
.SH REPOSITORY CONFIGURATION
Each *.conf file defines one repository in a [Repository] section. Its basename
is the repository ID. Files with the same basename override lower-precedence
files. The search order, from lowest to highest precedence, is:
.PP
/usr/lib/proto-sysext/repos.d/
.br
/usr/local/lib/proto-sysext/repos.d/
.br
/run/proto-sysext/repos.d/
.br
/etc/proto-sysext/repos.d/
.PP
A same-named symlink to /dev/null masks a repository. Root operations reject
non-root-owned or group/world-writable configuration files.
.TP
.B URL=
HTTPS repository base URL. Plain filenames are read from SHA256SUMS; directory
traversal, URL credentials, query strings and systemd specifiers are rejected.
.TP
.B Priority=
Integer from 0 to 999999; defaults to 99. Lower values are preferred.
.TP
.B Enabled=
Boolean, default yes.
.TP
.B KeyURL=
Optional HTTPS URL for public-key retrieval when the signer is not already
trusted. Defaults to repodata/repomd.xml.key below the repository URL. Only
one primary key may be returned, and it must actually verify the downloaded
listing before the user is asked to trust it. Keys are not stored in repository
configuration and are never accepted automatically.
.SH TRUST MODEL
The manager verifies repository listings with the same shared OpenPGP trust
store as systemd-sysupdate, including its version-dependent keyring precedence.
systemd 261 prefers a local keyring over the vendor ring; 262 combines them.
Effective public keyrings are imported into a temporary GnuPG home; automatic
signer/keyserver retrieval is disabled.
Initial image downloads and optional JSON files are checked against those
authenticated hashes. Embedded metadata must match ID, SYSEXT_LEVEL,
SYSEXT_VERSION_ID, SYSEXT_ID and architecture; metadata is never executed.
.PP
On first use, an unknown signer may be reviewed during an interactive root
refresh or install. The prompt displays the full primary fingerprint and the
repository/key URLs. This is trust-on-first-use: downloading the public key
from the same server is not independent authentication. A negative answer,
non-interactive input, key/signature mismatch, bad signature or multiple-key
response does not import anything. Approval preserves existing keys and adds
the accepted public key to /etc/systemd/import-pubring.pgp atomically.
.PP
Trust applies to all systemd imports, not just that repository. Any key in the
shared store may authorize any configured repository; this matches native
sysupdate semantics and is not per-repository key pinning. Configured repositories
must be trusted to supply privileged /usr content. Removing a repository does
not automatically revoke shared keys. Cached listings are reverified against
the current shared store whenever used.
.SH LOCKSTEP UPDATES
Images are named sysext-NAME-IMAGEID_IMAGEVERSION_ARCH.raw.zst. The image version
equals the OS IMAGE_VERSION. Composite independent extension versions are not
accepted. Tool versions remain in optional signed JSON. Published resources
for an OS version are immutable; extension-only rebuilds require a new OS release
to be offered as an update.
.PP
Generated definitions in /etc/sysupdate.d enable one optional feature per
extension. Transfers run before the UKI transfer, protect the running version
and retain two instances, aligned with Proto's A/B policy. Missing extensions
for an enabled feature prevent a newer OS version from forming a complete
update set. Existing sysupdated GUI workflows perform subsequent updates;
the manager does not run another updater or download timer.
.PP
systemd-sysext activates only extensions matching the running OS's SYSEXT_LEVEL.
The preset enables systemd-sysext.service for activation on boot. Explicit
installation/removal refreshes the current merge immediately.
.SH FILES
.TP
/var/cache/proto-sysext/REPO/URLHASH/
Disposable public catalog/signature cache, refreshed explicitly or during
installation. It is tied to the normalized URL, not installation ownership.
.TP
/etc/sysupdate.d/proto-sysext-*.feature
Owned feature definitions, retained when disabled.
.TP
/etc/sysupdate.d/65-proto-sysext-*.transfer
Owned native transfer definitions.
.TP
/var/lib/extensions/
Read-only downloaded extension images. Incompatible OS versions remain unmerged.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd-sysupdate (8),
.BR sysupdate.features (5),
.BR systemd-sysext (8),
.BR systemd-dissect (1)
